    "### Data Cropping\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Overview\n",
    "\n",
    "We agreed to do **Cluster-Based Cropping** on existing image. In this method:\n",
    "\n",
    "- We group insects that are close to each other based on their coordinates.\n",
    "- Then we crop one region around that group.\n",
    "- We calculate new bounding boxes inside the cropped image\n",
    "\n",
    "This method comes with some advantages:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Focuses only on areas** where insect exists, not on empty background.\n",
    "- **Keeps the local context** because nearby insects stay together.\n",
    "- Insects appear **larger and easier** for the model to detect.\n",
    "- Good for **increasing data for rare insect** species.\n",
    "\n",
    "However it also have some cons:\n",
    "\n",
    "- We have to choose the right distance threshold; if done wrong, insect **may grouped badly**.\n",
    "- Model may **lose understanding of the full image context**.\n",
    "\n",
    "#### Implementation\n",
    "\n",
    "Key steps:\n",
    "\n",
    "- Converts YOLO-normalized boxes to pixel coordinates. (Step 1)\n",
    "- Uses a simple greedy distance-based clustering on box centers. (Step 2–3) (Alternative is DBSCAN; probably needed with Utrecht data)\n",
    "- Builds a crop rectangle around each cluster, adds a margin, clamps to image bounds. (Step 4)\n",
    "- Convert rectangle to square (Step 5)\n",
    "- Crops the image and re calculate YOLO labels relative to the crop. (Step 6)\n",
    "- Saves cropped images and new .txt label files in an organized output folder. (Step 7)"
   ]
  },
